            <ArticleTitle>Associations between C-reactive protein and circulating cell adhesion molecules in patients with unstable angina undergoing coronary intervention and their clinical implication.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>47-51</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">There is growing evidence that C-reactive protein (CRP) may have a direct role in the pathogenesis of atherosclerosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="HYPOTHESIS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The purpose of this study was to assess associations between CRP and adhesion molecules and to determine the prognostic value of adhesion molecules as a predictor of cardiac events in patients with unstable angina.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Fifty-five consecutive patients (33 males, mean age 61 years) with unstable angina (Braunwald class IIb or IIIb) undergoing coronary stenting were included in this study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The test for a trend toward increasing intercellular adhesion molecule (ICAM)-1 concentrations by the 75th percentile of CRP levels at 72 h after coronary stenting was significant (p = 0.03). At 72 h after coronary stenting, CRP levels were the only determinants of ICAM-1 concentrations by multiple linear regression analysis. An elevated level of CRP (&gt;5.4 mg/l) (odds ratio [OR] 1.5, 95% confidence interval [CI] 1.3-3.7, p &lt; 0.05) and ICAM-1 (&gt;321 ng/ml) (OR 1.2, 95% CI 1.1-2.1, p &lt; 0.05) at 72 h after coronary stenting is an independent risk factor for an adverse cardiac event.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">These results suggest that in patients with unstable angina undergoing coronary stenting, the measurements of inflammatory parameters, especially CRP and ICAM-1, may be useful for identifying those at higher risk of a cardiac event, and CRP may play a direct role in promoting the inflammatory component of atherosclerosis by inducing significant expression of ICAM-1.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
